to-adam-chlipala-source : List Word
to-adam-chlipala-source = lines-of (cons "# Adam Chlipala"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Arthur J. Conner Professor of Computer Science, MIT CSAIL, Programming Languages and Verification group. Founder of Nectry; advisor to BlueRock Systems and SiFive."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Anything hyperactivity builds on from your work is credited to you in full. Each claim below about Avik Jain's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, Part I, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What your work established"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field treats formal verification as an audit: someone writes the fast code, and later someone else proves things about it, at a cost the industry mostly declines to pay. Your work rejects that order. In Fiat, a program is derived from its specification by refinement steps that each carry their proof. Fiat Cryptography applied this where it was supposedly impossible: the field-arithmetic code at the bottom of TLS, generated per prime and per machine word from one generic algorithm proved once in Coq. The output beat hand-tuned code and shipped in BoringSSL, so the connections of everyone using Chrome run through arithmetic whose correctness is a theorem."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Performance and correctness stop trading off when the compiler that produces the fast code also produces its proof. Kami carried the same discipline into hardware, with refinement proved module by module so that the proofs compose. Bedrock and Bedrock2 did it for low-level software, and the lightbulb and garage-door servers (PLDI 2021, PLDI 2024) joined them into single theorems running from the application's behavior through the compiler down to the processor's specification. Ur/Web rules out whole classes of web vulnerability by the type of the program, and *Certified Programming with Dependent Types* taught a generation that the proof assistant is a programming environment."
  (cons ""
  (cons "What your work shows, more clearly than anyone else's, is that a specification, its implementation at every level of the stack, and the evidence connecting them can be one object. The field still prices this as an add-on for exceptional code. Your results say it belongs to the definition of compilation."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes up"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Hyperactivity is a language and a computational model of reality whose computation rules are the CCHM cubical calculus with clock-indexed guarded computation"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ". A requirement on a function is a chosen dependent family, and the reasoning establishing it is a term of the language; expressing that reasoning in the language of the construction preserves it for further use instead of translating it into a separate verification model"
  (cons (cite s1-p25 "(1.5)")
  (cons (cite s1-p26 "(§1)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Specialization returns its justification.** The paper's specializer takes a represented term and returns a new code together with a code for its justification: normalization yields a representation convertible to the input, and theorem-directed rewriting yields an explicit path between the interpreted terms, which the checker must accept, so a wrong theorem application cannot be admitted"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p6 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ". The residual is checked at its declared type before it is admitted as a definition"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p3 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ". Applied to an interpreter and a source program, and to the specializer itself, the same operations give the Futamura constructions, with no new primitive for compilers"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p11 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ". Your one generic proof covering every specialization is the case this mechanism is built for."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Two relations, kept distinct.** Accepting a specialized term by conversion or a checked path establishes its relation to the source definition; the meaning of emitted bytes under the target's instruction semantics is a separate source-to-machine construction"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p17 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ". Making a machine a realization of a source takes an explicit simulation or refinement relating their actual observations"
  (cons (cite s9-p2 "(§9)")
  (cons ", and the paper proves a structural compiler theorem whose stack-extension invariant holds under arbitrary surrounding stack contents"
  (cons (cite s9-3-p18 "(§9.3)")
  (cons ": the compositional form your end-to-end theorems need at each layer."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Through C, by composing relations.** The primary target is C: a program, or a specialization of its evaluator at known inputs, is lowered to C together with its relation to the source, and an existing C compiler supplies instruction selection"
  (cons (cite s9-4-p2 "(§9.4)")
  (cons "; the source-to-binary claim is the composition of the two relations"
  (cons (cite s9-4-p3 "(§9.4)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Proofs are stored by content.** A content-addressed codebase holds programs together with their typing derivations, equivalences, dependency certificates and reusable proofs, and an edit's effect on dependent definitions is computed by the same dependency operations used for persistent evaluation"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". After an edit, a proof whose statement mentions the old program does not silently become a proof about the new one; its dependent type changes and must be inhabited again"
  (cons (cite s10-11-p6 "(§10.11)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## The line from protocol parsers to hardware"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Avik came to this through the problem your work addresses. At Citadel Securities, with Gašper Ažman, he built a language for arbitrary network protocols. It generated optimal reactive C++ parser libraries, template-only and further optimized by the compiler for each wiring of hooks, intended to compile down to NIC and FPGA code. That is a high-level specification compiled into the fastest artifact the hardware permits, the same problem as Fiat Cryptography and Kami in another domain. What that work lacked was what yours supplies: the artifact carrying its proof."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Credit"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Every piece of your work that hyperactivity builds on is credited to you in full: deriving programs by proof-carrying refinement, proof-producing specialization of generic code, modular refinement for hardware, end-to-end integration verification, and the proof assistant as a programming environment. Nothing here supersedes it. Read the rest of the work for the places where your results become properties of a language."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Adam Chlipala, home page, https://adam.chlipala.net"
  (cons "- Fiat Cryptography, S&P 2019, https://adam.chlipala.net/papers/FiatCryptoSP19/"
  (cons "- Kami, ICFP 2017, https://adam.chlipala.net/papers/KamiICFP17/"
  (cons "- Foundational Integration Verification of a Cryptographic Server, PLDI 2024, https://adam.chlipala.net/papers/GarageDoorPLDI24/"
  (cons "- MIT CSAIL profile, https://cap.csail.mit.edu/members/people/adam-chlipala"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I, the blocks linked above"
  nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
